The president of the Republic of Bulgaria ( [LINK: (Bulgarian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_language] : Президент на Република България,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization] : Prezident na Republika Bălgariya) is the  [LINK: (head of state) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_of_state]  of  [LINK: (Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgaria]  and the  [LINK: (commander-in-chief) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commander-in-chief]  of the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Armed Forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Armed_Forces] . The official residence of the president is at Boyana Residence,  [LINK: (Sofia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sofia] .

In  [LINK: (Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgaria]  the main function of the president is to be the  [LINK: (arbitrator) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbitrator]  of disputes between Bulgaria's different institutions. They are not considered  [LINK: (head of government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_of_government]  or part of the nation's  [LINK: (executive power) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_power] . However, in the absence of a prime minister, presidents are in charge of appointing an interim administration, giving them considerable influence over the government during such periods ( [LINK: (Zhelyu Zhelev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhelyu_Zhelev]  in 1994-95;  [LINK: (Petar Stoyanov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petar_Stoyanov]  in 1997;  [LINK: (Rosen Plevneliev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosen_Plevneliev]  in 2013 and 2014;  [LINK: (Rumen Radev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev]  in 2017, 2021, and since 2022; and  [LINK: (Iliana Iotova) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova]  in 2026). On some occasions, the president has appointed the  [LINK: (prime minister) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_Minister_of_Bulgaria]  as well. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-2]

The president is elected for a five-year term and is restricted to two terms, even if they are non-successive. After an individual has served two terms as president, that individual will forever be barred from being elected to the presidency again under the rules set out by Bulgaria's Constitution. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-:0-3]  The president addresses the nation on national television annually on  [LINK: (New Year's Eve) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Year%27s_Eve] , just moments before the start of the new year. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-4]


# ELECTION #

For a Bulgarian citizen to be able to run for the office of President of Bulgaria, they must fulfil the following conditions: [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-:0-3]

Must be a Bulgarian citizen by birth

Must be at least 40 years old

Must have lived in Bulgaria for the five years prior to the candidacy

Must fulfil all conditions that would be required for election as a representative to Bulgaria's National Assembly

The president is elected directly by the Bulgarian people in a  [LINK: (two-round) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Two-round_system]   [LINK: (majoritarian election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Majoritarian] . If a candidate manages to obtain more than 50% of the vote and the  [LINK: (voter turnout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voter_turnout]  was at least 50% in the first round, that candidate is elected. If no candidate manages to obtain more than 50% of the vote or the voter turnout was lower than 50% in the first round, then the two top-performing candidates face off in a second round with  [LINK: (first-past-the-post voting) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First-past-the-post_voting] , with the candidate receiving the larger number of votes considered elected. [LINK: ([a]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-5]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-:0-3]


# RESTRICTIONS #

The president is banned from also being a member of the  [LINK: (National Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Assembly_(Bulgaria)] , as well taking on any other government, public or private offices for the duration of his term. The president is also constitutionally forbidden from being involved in a leadership position of a  [LINK: (political party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_party]  while in office. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-:0-3]

In practice, despite the fact that most candidates for president are elected from a political party's list [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-6]  and despite the fact that the Constitution doesn't forbid the president from being an ordinary member of a political party, it is widely expected in Bulgarian society that the president be above politics. For this reason, the president-elect is expected to give up any membership in a political party. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-7]  [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-8]  [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-9]


# POWERS AND PRIVILEGES #

The president of Bulgaria has a number of functions and powers that are regulated in Chapter 4 of the 1991  [LINK: (Constitution of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitution_of_Bulgaria] . The president is elected directly by a  [LINK: (popular vote) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct_election]  for a period of five years which is renewable.

The following powers belong to the president of Bulgaria: [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-:0-3]

The ability to give, restore or revoke [LINK: ([b]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-10]   [LINK: (Bulgarian citizenship) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_citizenship] , and  [LINK: (refugee status) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Refugee_status] .

Appoint and dismiss senior government officials

Exercise the right to  [LINK: (pardon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pardon]  convicted criminals, as well as forgive 'uncollectible' debts owed to the government

Rename villages, towns and cities, as well as objects of national importance

Act as Supreme commander-in-chief of the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Armed Forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Armed_Forces]

Represent Bulgaria at home and abroad

Schedule the National and local elections within the intervals designated by law

Veto any bill coming from the National Assembly by refusing to sign it after its passage in the assembly. [LINK: ([c]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-11]

Declares war,  [LINK: (martial law) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martial_law]  or any other  [LINK: (state of emergency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_of_emergency]  (This is done with the assistance of the Consultative Council on National Security)

Grant and award the  [LINK: (orders, decorations, and medals of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orders,_decorations,_and_medals_of_Bulgaria]

The president enjoys blanket  [LINK: (legal immunity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_immunity]  during his or her tenure and is not held responsible for any act performed while on duty, with the exception of treason or violation of the Bulgarian constitution. His or her authority may only be stripped via impeachment and may not be removed by any other institution. The president cannot be  [LINK: (detained) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Detention_(imprisonment)]  and may not be prosecuted. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-:0-3]


# LIST OF PRESIDENTS OF BULGARIA (1990–PRESENT) #

(1964–)


# LIVING FORMER PRESIDENTS #

The most recent death of a former president was that of  [LINK: (Zhelyu Zhelev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhelyu_Zhelev]  (1990–1997), who died on January 30, 2015 at the age of 79.


# VICE PRESIDENT #

The president is assisted in these duties by the  [LINK: (vice president of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vice_President_of_Bulgaria] . The vice president replaces the president in case of absence. Only upon the death, resignation, removal from office or disability of the president,  the vice president assumes the presidential powers & duties, until elections are held. The Constitution permits the president to delegate to the vice president the powers to appoint and dismiss certain officials, issue pardons and amnesty, provide citizenship and refugee status, but does not allow the president to delegate any of his other powers. He or she enjoys the same privileges of immunity as the president and can only be dismissed from his office under the same procedures as those regarding the president. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-:0-3]


# TERMINATION OF OFFICE #

According to the constitution, the mandate of the president is completed if and when: [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-:0-3]

The presidential term expires

The president resigns before the Constitutional Court

The president is permanently unable to perform his/her duties due to serious illness

The president dies while in office

The president is  [LINK: (impeached) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impeachment]

[LINK: (Impeachment) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impeachment]  can only begin if the president has committed  [LINK: (treason) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treason]  or has violated the  [LINK: (Constitution of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitution_of_Bulgaria] . Impeachment starts after at least a quarter of the members of the National Assembly deposit an accusatory act before the assembly. The act must then be approved by a  [LINK: (supermajority) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supermajority]  of 2/3 of all elected representatives in order to be accepted. If accepted, the case is referred to the  [LINK: (Constitutional Court of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitutional_Court_of_Bulgaria] , which must decide within a one-month time span whether or not the president is guilty of the crime he has been accused of by the Assembly. If the constitutional court finds that the president has committed treason or violated the constitution, as per the accusatory act, then the president is considered successfully impeached and is stripped of his authority. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria#cite_note-:0-3]


